      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the number among the given list (1, 2, 3, ..., 15) that has the highest number of divisors.\n\nSince prime numbers have only one divisor, we should start with identifying all prime numbers below 15. Then, we'll check how many divisors each of these prime numbers has.\n\n1. List all prime numbers below 15:\n    - 2 (divisors: 1, 2)\n    - 3 (divisors: 1, 3)\n    - 5 (divisors: 1, 5)\n    - 7 (divisors: 1, 7)\n    - 11 (divisors: 1, 11)\n    - 13 (divisors: 1, 13)\n\n2. List all composite numbers in the range (15 is not included):\n    - 4 (divisors: 1, 2, 4)\n    - 6 (divisors: 1, 2, 3, 6)\n    - 8 (divisors: 1, 2, 4, 8)\n    - 9 (divisors: 1, 3, 9)\n    - 10 (divisors: 1, 2, 5, 10)\n    - 12 (divisors: 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12)\n    - 14 (divisors: 1, 2, 7, 14)\n\n3. Count the number of divisors for each of the prime numbers found.\n\n    - 2: Divisors = 2\n    - 3: Divisors = 2\n    - 5: Divisors = 2\n    - 7: Divisors = 2\n    - 11: Divisors = 2\n    - 13: Divisors = 2\n\nWe observe that the number of divisors for each prime number is the same (2). Since both 4 and 6 also have 2 divisors, we can check if there are more divisors among these numbers.\n\n4. List the number corresponding to these composite numbers:\n    - 4 (divisors: 2)\n    - 6 (divisors: 3)\n    - 8 (divisors: 4)\n    - 9 (divisors: 3)\n    - 10 (divisors: 4)\n    - 12 (divisors: 6)\n    - 14 (divisors: 4)\n\n5. Determine which composite number has the most divisors from our newly listed composite numbers, which include 4, 6, 8, 9, 10, 12, and 14. The number with the maximum count of divisors is 12.\n\nThe number with the greatest number of divisors among 1, 2, 3, ..., 15 is 12, with a total of 6 divisors.\n\nThus, the answer to the problem is $\\boxed{12}$.",
